; LIMIT 07: lib/io.l0 throws away what the builtins say about failure.
;
; Costly in programs/kvstore, in two ways:
;
; 1. IoFailed carries only words. kv treats a store that does not exist yet
;    as empty but must report permission denied, so it compares the message
;    with the text io_error happens to use, "no such file". Rewording that
;    message in the library would silently turn every first kv set into an
;    error. One line each in load and cmd_get, but coupled to prose.
;
; 2. file_read_line returns what it has read on any negative result, so a
;    read that fails partway looks like the end of the file. kv rewrites the
;    store after reading it, and would write back only the entries before
;    the failure, losing the rest with exit status 0. kv.l0 has its own line
;    reader over file_read_byte instead: the Line, Lines, and Scan types and
;    next_line, read_lines, and scan_for, about 40 lines.
;
; No checker error; both are library behaviour. This program shows the
; first: it can tell "missing" from "unreadable" only by the message text.

(def main () int
  (match (open_in (arg 0))
    ((IoFailed why)
      (if (list_eq why "no such file")
          (let w (write_line "missing, which is fine") 0)
          (let w (write_line (append "cannot open: " why)) 2)))
    ((IoOpened f)
      (let c (file_close f)
        (let w (write_line "exists") 0)))))
